---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Search from '../components/Search.astro';

// The single canonical FAQPage for the site. The homepage and /emergency used
// to carry their own FAQPage blocks describing content that was not visible on
// those pages, a structured-data policy violation, and three competing answers
// to the same question. Those were removed; this is the one source of truth.
//
// Every entry carries a `fix` link. The page copy has always promised "every
// answer links to the full fix" and used to link nothing; between them these
// entries now link all 12 /fix articles, which also de-orphans the two that
// had zero inbound links.
const faqs = [
  {
    id: 'usb-not-detected',
    q: 'Why is my USB not detected on a Pioneer CDJ or XDJ?',
    a: 'The most common causes, in order: the drive is formatted as NTFS or exFAT instead of FAT32; the partition map is GPT (we recommend MBR); the rekordbox database on the drive is corrupt (often from an unsafe eject); or the drive hardware is failing. You have already reseated it and tried the other player, so start from the file system: most "dead" drives are a wrong format, a GPT map or a corrupt database, not dead hardware.',
    fix: { href: '/fix/usb-not-recognized-cdj', label: 'Full fix: CDJ not reading your USB' },
  },
  {
    id: 'e-8302',
    q: 'What does CDJ error E-8302 mean?',
    a: 'On the CDJ-3000 and CDJ-3000X, E-8302 reads "CANNOT PLAY TRACK": the player cannot read the track data on the drive, in most cases a corrupt rekordbox export or a wrong file system. Those two players are the ones AlphaTheta documents the code for, and DJs report it on earlier CDJ-2000 units too, where it means the same thing. With a laptop you can usually fix it on-site: re-export fresh from rekordbox, or, once everything on the drive that exists nowhere else is copied off, reformat FAT32/MBR and re-export. Never format a drive that still holds your only copy of anything.',
    fix: { href: '/fix/cdj-error-e-8302', label: 'Full fix: CDJ error E-8302' },
  },
  {
    id: 'format-usb',
    q: 'How do I format a USB for CDJs?',
    a: 'FAT32 file system, MBR (Master Boot Record) partition scheme. On Mac: Disk Utility → Show All Devices → select the drive (not the volume) → Erase → MS-DOS (FAT) + Master Boot Record. On Windows the built-in Format dialog only offers FAT32 up to 32 GB; for a 64 GB drive use the free guiformat tool, the full walkthrough is linked below. Use a quality 32-64 GB drive.',
    fix: { href: '/fix/format-usb-for-cdj', label: 'Full fix: format a USB for CDJs, step by step' },
  },
  {
    id: 'exfat-or-fat32',
    q: 'Should I use exFAT or FAT32 for DJing?',
    a: 'FAT32, unless you know every player you will touch reads exFAT. FAT32 works on every rekordbox-era Pioneer DJ and AlphaTheta player, old and new. exFAT is only read by newer units on recent firmware, so a drive that works on a CDJ-3000 can be refused by the CDJ-2000NXS2 in the next booth. FAT32 is the format that never surprises you.',
    fix: { href: '/fix/exfat-vs-fat32-cdj', label: 'Full fix: exFAT vs FAT32 on CDJs' },
  },
  {
    id: 'not-exported',
    q: 'Can I play tracks that were not exported with rekordbox?',
    a: 'Yes. CDJs can play plain audio files from a FAT32 USB via the FOLDER view. You lose cues, grids, playlists, Sync and Quantize, but you keep the gig. That is the core of our critical-path rescue.',
    fix: { href: '/emergency', label: 'Start Emergency Mode' },
  },
  {
    id: 'export-shows-nothing',
    q: 'My export finished but the player shows nothing. Why?',
    a: 'The database landed corrupt, classic after pulling the drive without ejecting. First, in rekordbox, open Preferences, DJ System, Device and check that Delete Tracks is OFF. With it on, deleting a playlist from the device also deletes the audio of every track no other playlist on that drive uses. Then delete the playlists from the device, re-sync them fresh, and eject with the rekordbox eject button. If the device errors, copy your music off, format clean, export again.',
    fix: { href: '/fix/playlists-not-showing-cdj', label: 'Full fix: playlists not showing on the CDJ' },
  },
  {
    id: 'export-failed',
    q: 'What do I do when rekordbox says the export failed?',
    a: 'Work in order. Check there is free space on the drive. Then open Preferences, DJ System, Device and check that Delete Tracks is OFF, because with it on, deleting a playlist from the device also deletes the audio of every track no other playlist on that drive uses. Now delete the device playlists, re-sync fresh, and eject with the rekordbox eject button. If the export keeps failing, the drive itself is usually the problem, copy the music off and reformat FAT32/MBR before exporting again.',
    fix: { href: '/fix/rekordbox-export-failed', label: 'Full fix: rekordbox export failed' },
  },
  {
    id: 'rekordbox-no-usb',
    q: 'Why is rekordbox not detecting my USB?',
    a: 'If the operating system sees the drive but rekordbox does not, it is almost always the file system or the partition map, not the software. Confirm the drive is FAT32 with an MBR scheme, then restart rekordbox with the drive already connected.',
    fix: { href: '/fix/rekordbox-not-detecting-usb', label: 'Full fix: rekordbox not detecting the USB' },
  },
  {
    id: 'waveforms',
    q: 'Why are waveforms not loading on my CDJ?',
    a: 'The audio is on the drive but the analysis data is missing or unreadable. Usually the tracks were copied manually instead of exported from rekordbox, or the export was interrupted. Re-analyse in rekordbox and export properly and the waveforms come back.',
    fix: { href: '/fix/waveforms-not-loading-cdj', label: 'Full fix: waveforms not loading' },
  },
  {
    id: 'emergency-loop',
    q: 'What is Emergency Loop mode?',
    a: 'Official behaviour: the player automatically plays a Beat Loop if it cannot start the next track when the end of the current one is reached, so the room never goes silent. If nothing was loaded, that is normal. If it fires when your next track was ready, the player could not read it: treat the drive as suspect and mix out on the other player.',
    fix: { href: '/fix/emergency-loop-mode', label: 'Full fix: Emergency Loop mode' },
  },
  {
    id: 'how-many-drives',
    q: 'How many USB drives should I bring to a gig?',
    a: 'Two, identical, with the same export on both. A second drive is the only backup that works when you are already standing in the booth, and it turns a dead gig into a ten-second swap.',
    fix: { href: '/fix/dj-usb-backup-strategy', label: 'Full fix: the DJ USB backup strategy' },
  },
  {
    id: 'usb-size',
    q: 'What size USB drive is best for DJing?',
    a: '32-64 GB is the sweet spot. Big enough for a full working library, small enough that FAT32 formats without a fight. Bigger drives are slower to format, slower to scan and no more useful for a single gig.',
    fix: { href: '/fix/best-usb-size-for-djing', label: 'Full fix: how big should a DJ USB be' },
  },
  {
    id: 'new-laptop',
    q: 'How do I move my rekordbox library to a new laptop without losing cues?',
    a: 'Use rekordbox\'s own Backup Library with "include music files" checked, then restore that backup on the new machine. Copying only the audio files loses every cue, grid and playlist, which is the mistake almost everyone makes once.',
    fix: { href: '/fix/move-rekordbox-library-new-laptop', label: 'Full fix: move rekordbox to a new laptop' },
  },
  {
    id: 'works-offline',
    q: 'Does Save My Gig work without internet?',
    a: 'The rescue kit does, by design. On your first visit the site saves the emergency start page, every step of all four rescue flows, the checklist, the pocket card, the rekordbox reference, the dictionary and search onto your device, and from then on those load with no signal at all. Conditions: you must have opened the site once while online, and clearing your browser data clears the kit until the next online visit. Adding it to your home screen protects the stored kit best, especially on iPhone.',
    fix: { href: '/install', label: 'How to install it, on every device' },
  },
  {
    id: 'is-it-free',
    q: 'Is Save My Gig really free?',
    a: 'Yes. No account and no paywall, ever. If it saves your gig you can buy us a coffee, but only after you are safe.',
    fix: { href: '/about', label: 'The story behind Save My Gig' },
  },
  {
    id: 'which-drives',
    q: 'Which USB drives should I buy for DJing?',
    a: 'A quality metal-bodied 32-64 GB stick from a major brand, and buy two identical ones, always. Our gear page lists the exact models we trust.',
    fix: { href: '/gear', label: 'See the gear we trust' },
  },
];

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/faq#faq',
  about: { '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/#org' },
  mainEntity: faqs.map(f => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: f.q,
    url: `https://www.savemygig.com/faq#${f.id}`,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
  })),
};
---
<Base
  title="DJ USB & CDJ FAQ - Every Question, Answered | Save My Gig"
  description="USB not detected, E-8302, rekordbox exports, FAT32 formatting, emergency loops, the questions every DJ asks, answered by people who have lived them."
  path="/faq"
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" slot="head" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />
  <main class="wrap wrap-wide">
    <div class="status-line">FAQ</div>
    <h1 style="margin-bottom:0.5rem">QUESTIONS FROM THE <span class="accent">BOOTH</span></h1>
    <p class="dim" style="margin-bottom:1.2rem">Short answers first. Every answer links to the full fix.</p>

    <div style="margin-bottom:2rem"><Search /></div>

    <!-- Collapsed by default (Antonio's sitewide rule for long text): the
         questions are the index, the person expands the answer they came for.
         FAQPage schema and search engines still get every answer, it is all in
         the HTML. -->
    {faqs.map(f => (
      <details class="panel faq-sec" id={f.id}>
        <summary>
          <h2 class="faq-q">{f.q}</h2>
          <span class="faq-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </summary>
        <div class="acc-body">
          <p class="dim faq-a">{f.a}</p>
          <a href={f.fix.href} class="faq-fix">{f.fix.label} &rarr;</a>
        </div>
      </details>
    ))}

    <div class="panel cta-exit" style="margin-top:2rem">
      <h2 style="margin-bottom:0.75rem;font-size:1.15rem">Question is urgent because you play soon?</h2>
      <a href="/emergency" class="btn btn-quiet-danger" data-event="begin_checkout" data-placement="faq"><span aria-hidden="true">🚨</span> Start Emergency Mode</a>
    </div>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  /* Design language sprint, 2026-08-03: the summary layout, the chevron, the
     focus ring and the open state used to be redeclared here, a copy of the
     same code in the dictionary, the Pioneer hub and Detail.astro. They now
     live once in global.css under the shared accordion. Only the question's
     own typography is local, because only that is specific to the FAQ. */
  .faq-q { font-size: 1.15rem; font-weight: 800; margin: 0; letter-spacing: -0.01em; }
  /* .faq-sec inherits .panel's surface; the shared accordion supplies the
     padding on the summary and .acc-body supplies it on the answer, so the
     panel's own must not double up. */
  .faq-sec { padding: 0; }
</style>

<script is:inline>
  // Deep links (search results and #anchors) open the closed answer first.
  (function () {
    function openTarget() {
      var id = location.hash.slice(1);
      if (!id) return;
      var el = document.getElementById(id);
      if (el && el.tagName === 'DETAILS' && !el.open) el.open = true;
      if (el) el.scrollIntoView();
    }
    window.addEventListener('hashchange', openTarget);
    openTarget();
  })();
</script>

<style>
  .faq-a { margin-bottom: 0.8rem; }
  .faq-fix {
    font-family: var(--font-body); font-weight: 600; font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--red); text-decoration: none;
  }
  .faq-fix:hover { text-decoration: underline; }
  /* Anchor landing shouldn't hide the question under the top of the viewport. */
  .panel[id] { scroll-margin-top: 1.5rem; }
</style>
